findings.body:
The fallback for `.scrollToSelectedTab` when no tab is selected used to return `state.tabs.count - 1`, which is the last tab. This PR changes it to `state.tabs.count`, which is one past the end of the array.

The `ScrollState.toIndex` is turned into `IndexPath(row: scrollState.toIndex, section: 1)` in `TabDisplayView.scrollToTab`, and that index path is passed to the collection view's scroll API. With the index past the last item, the collection view will either throw or scroll to a nonexistent item. This is a crash or a broken scroll on the common path where the user switches between the normal and private tab panels.

The unit test `testCreateTabScrollBehavior_forScrollToSelectedTab_noSelectedTab_returnsLastTab_ifTabsNotEmpty` still asserts `tabCount - 1` (`TabsPanelStateTests.swift` line 152), so it will fail once this change is compiled and run. The XCUI changes also don't cover this path.

The fix is to restore `state.tabs.count - 1`, which the existing test and its comment already expect.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Client/Frontend/Browser/Tabs/State/TabsPanelState.swift" lines="138-146"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The PR appears to have been merged without running the scroll-behavior tests, because they fail on this line.

::suggestion{file="firefox-ios/Client/Frontend/Browser/Tabs/State/TabsPanelState.swift" lines="143-143"}
                return ScrollState(toIndex: state.tabs.count - 1, withAnimation: shouldAnimate)

`TabTrayStateTests.swift` still has `testRefreshInactiveTabsAction`, which calls `getTabPanelMiddleWareAction(for: .refreshInactiveTabs)`. This PR deletes `case refreshInactiveTabs` from `TabPanelMiddlewareActionType`, so the test target can't compile. That blocks every test in ClientTests, not just this one.

The PR's own test cleanup missed this file, which suggests the test target was not built after the change. The fix is to delete `testRefreshInactiveTabsAction` in `TabTrayStateTests.swift` (around line 142-154). Its only purpose was to exercise the removed action.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Client/Frontend/Browser/Tabs/Action/TabPanelAction.swift" lines="85-91"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The deleted enum case is the reason the test no longer compiles. The test file itself isn't in this diff, so the fix has to happen there.

The first three lines of `TabPanelAction.swift` are the Mozilla Public License header. The PR removes them, but every other Swift source in this area keeps it, including `TabDisplayView.swift` and `TabsPanelState.swift`. The repo's license-header linting and convention both expect it, so this will be flagged by CI or by a reviewer.

This looks like an accidental edit from the removal work, since the other hunks in this file are about inactive tabs. Restore the three-line header.

::diff{file="firefox-ios/Client/Frontend/Browser/Tabs/Action/TabPanelAction.swift" lines="1-3"}

The PR moves the four inactive-tab strings out of `TabsTray.InactiveTabs` into a new `struct v147` nested in a Strings extension. After this PR, none of these properties (`CloseAllInactiveTabsButton`, `CloseInactiveTabSwipeActionTitle`, and the two accessibility titles) is referenced anywhere in the Swift sources. The struct is also not `public`, unlike every neighbouring struct in the file, and the `v147` name doesn't describe what it holds.

If the goal is to keep localized keys around for the l10n pipeline, that should be documented. Otherwise these are dead code that the cleanup PR was meant to remove. The `TabsTrayInactiveTabsSectionTitle` string around line 7879 is still in the file too, so the leftover surface is larger than this block.

